ion/math-academy-id "q-93729" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are real?\n\n1. $\\sqrt{2}$\n2. $-\\sqrt{4}$\n3. $\\sqrt{-2}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050130 :topic/math-academy-id 2129 :topic/title "QR Factorization"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be78f97c-a223-5ab6-b494-bf9064deb166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133315} {:answer/id #uuid "076084fc-5240-5f13-bc41-f4207752e1b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133316} {:answer/id #uuid "1dcf723b-8f97-5504-88a1-7f4a31a1e4bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133317} {:answer/id #uuid "32bf15c7-9ae6-5051-b497-8d314f8c95a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133318} {:answer/id #uuid "41151fbb-e388-5e0f-828b-a2e497a4ca4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133319}] :answer-field/id #uuid "af4ec3ba-fc26-5e37-bc3f-2f09063c1d7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133314}] :question/id #uuid "b75151e1-73fe-588a-be1a-8c3a00e71cfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-95165" :question/problem "Given that $A = QR$ is a QR factorization of a $4 \\cdot 3$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The columns of $Q$ are orthogonal\n2. $Q$ is a $4 \\cdot 3$ matrix\n3. $R$ is a $4 \\cdot 3$ matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1304189f-61ac-53c5-9b09-8e365f763136" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Dimensions of Factors in a QR Factorization" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050130 :topic/math-academy-id 2129 :topic/title "QR Factorization"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7bf8b1d-e78c-57fc-bfaf-91fa5873cf7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133327} {:answer/id #uuid "183524dc-bb03-557b-88e8-28da20de1079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133328} {:answer/id #uuid "38cb179a-1edc-59f1-aae2-22d35d515ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133329} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba29114-d76f-533f-b8cf-6f1e588e26b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133330} {:answer/id #uuid "79dce91f-eb3d-5e87-ac7e-f84e69cea966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133331}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a41bcd7c-8918-595d-a914-0f4cd48fa388" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133326} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "159cd27a-ea9a-5537-8d36-8696601ef2e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364566} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c3d583-3281-5912-9d32-99fa0594c1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364567} {:answer/id #uuid "0d75889c-787c-5d9c-a532-5b3c260fe26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364568} {:answer/id #uuid "7afd7fce-a080-5c3d-9c16-0f2ea800ced4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364569} {:answer/id #uuid "0a13ab10-a592-5f0c-aa2f-6642dfe24354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d75889c-787c-5d9c-a532-5b3c260fe26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364568} :answer-field/id #uuid "67537453-022d-5eff-b19d-955bb75fa807" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84614411-01cd-5a8c-8ef0-a58925fca35b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95915" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $Q$ is a square matrix\n- $Q$ is an upper triangular matrix\n- $R$ is an upper triangular matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a  QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be an $m\\times n$ matrix, so $Q$ is not necessarily a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. $Q$ should be an orthogonal matrix, not necessarily an upper triangular matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $R$ must be an upper triangular matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/math-academy-id 2104 :topic/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15066878-30bb-508c-9db7-e8b10b72c732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133453} {:answer/id #uuid "1c2b7f86-e92f-5e2c-8860-4730a35f5501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133454} {:answer/id #uuid "29f32be0-6e29-59ac-a011-e3b3dd051fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133455} {:answer/id #uuid "73574aac-cbae-5bc4-ab25-5412b8255db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133456} {:answer/id #uuid "7a14430d-daac-5eb0-95da-dfa439a6fb71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133457}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3e7eb080-b1fd-507d-8288-fb67ef87f7d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133452}] :question/id #uuid "a07c5a93-5161-56bc-aa10-7d11733928e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-99808" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n$f_{1}(t) = t^{2}, f_{2}(t) = t^{3}, f_{3}(t) = t$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[- 1, 1]$ of continuous functions over $[- 1, 1]$ is defined as\n$⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = \\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)dt$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f_{1}(t)⊥f_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $f_{2}(t)⊥f_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S = {f_{1}(t), f_{2}(t), f_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨f_{1}, f_{3}⟩ = 0$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138555 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "658cbf1b-ca35-5d86-82e6-638f18277212" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Numbers Containing Zeros Written in Expanded Form" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049669 :topic/math-academy-id 2311 :topic/title "Converting From Expanded Form to Standard Form"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3f2c4ea-84ca-5361-b0dc-189c618e3e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135344} {:answer/id #uuid "e274929b-9a54-588a-a14f-13ff40bd76ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135345} {:answer/id #uuid "19e0a08e-3050-5208-92a6-cd7996613dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135346} {:answer/id #uuid "28e28d13-f249-53bc-97cb-ba55d0bc89ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135347} {:answer/id #uuid "3afb2eff-032a-53ad-bc89-173d85389de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135348}] :answer